-- hypermath verify.hm
-- The hypermath self-verification suite.
-- A set of formally stated rules that every .hm file must satisfy
-- for the hypermath_form system to count as self-verifying.
--
-- Presupposes: axioms.hm (primitives, relations, axioms, quant family, +, additionally)
--              casting.hm (cast, FORM/FRAME from casting, cond, <=)
--
-- Status: FORM for the rule definitions.
--         FRAME for the kernel implementation (a running checker does not yet exist).
--
-- The critical structural fact:
-- verify.hm must survive its own rules.
-- This is not a claim — it is a test.
-- If verify.hm violates any rule it defines, that is a contradiction
-- to be surfaced immediately, not silenced.
-- Each rule below is followed by a check of whether this file passes it.

-- ================================================================
-- § 0. Why a Self-Verification Suite
-- ================================================================
--
-- The □-system closes by re-application: cast(□, A) =~ A is FORM.
-- A formal system that cannot verify itself is FRAME at the system level.
-- It articulates structure but does not survive □-casting as a whole.
-- The self-verification suite is the casting operation applied to
-- hypermath_form as a single form: the entire spec cast through □,
-- with the rules being the □-shape that must be retained.
--
-- This is not an external test harness. It is the system applying
-- its own derivation rules to itself. The kernel that enforces these
-- rules is the caster. The .hm files are the material.
-- FORM at the system level means: the system cast through its own rules
-- returns the same system, at =~ or above.

-- ================================================================
-- § 1. Rule Inventory
-- ================================================================

-- RULE 1: CLOSE-LINE
-- Every derive block must end with a close: relation(X) line.
-- X must be one of: relation(~~), relation(=~), relation(==).
-- A derive block without a close line is a half-cast: the form
-- is articulated but the casting result is not stated.
-- Status: FORM — this file satisfies it (all derive blocks below have close lines).

-- RULE 2: FRAME-NAMING
-- Every FRAME must be explicitly named with:
--   (a) what is currently absent (the open gap),
--   (b) what would close it (the closure criterion),
--   (c) why closure is not yet available (the honest status).
-- A FRAME without all three is not honestly labeled — it is a FRAME
-- claiming to be more closed than it is.
-- Status: FORM — this file's FRAMEs are named in § 0 header and § 6 below.

-- RULE 3: FILTRATION-INTEGRITY
-- No derive block may close at relation(==) unless its derivation
-- steps have established =~ along the way.
-- No derive block may close at relation(=~) unless its derivation
-- steps have established ~~ along the way.
-- Claiming == from a ~~-derivation is a kernel error.
-- The filtration is an orbit (axioms.hm § 1b): you cannot skip positions.
-- Status: FORM — every close line in this file is earned by the steps above it.

-- RULE 4: ETC — ETYMOLOGICAL TERM CLARITY
-- Every new term introduced in a .hm file must carry, on its first appearance,
-- a comment block deriving its meaning from its own parts (etymology).
-- The test: a reader who knows English but no prior formalism must be able
-- to approximate the meaning from the comment alone.
-- Opaque symbols without etymological grounding are FRAME:
-- they carry the appearance of formalism without the derivable structure.
-- Exception: □, ~~, =~, == — these are irreducible and are glossed in axioms.hm.
-- All other symbols and coined terms require ETC on first use.
-- Status: FORM — all terms in this file are derived from English roots in context.

-- RULE 5: NO-IMPORT-WITHOUT-DERIVATION
-- No symbol, operation, or structure from classical mathematics may appear
-- in a .hm file unless it has been explicitly derived from □ in a prior file.
-- Borrowing classical constructs without derivation is importing a foundation
-- alongside the □-foundation — two foundations is not a closed system.
-- PERMITTED: +, <=, quant, ordinatic forms — all derived in axioms.hm or casting.hm.
-- NOT PERMITTED: ∈ (set membership), ∀/∃ (classical quantifiers),
--                ℕ (Peano naturals), classical if-then without cond.
-- Status: FORM — this file uses only □-derived operations.

-- RULE 6: QUANT-SWEEP
-- No .hm file may use a bare ranging variable n, i, j, k for finite counts.
-- The □-derived ranging variable is quant (axioms.hm § 6).
-- Specific measured values are quanta_1, quanta_2, ... etc.
-- n is a classical Peano artifact. quant is derived from for(m) etymology.
-- Replacing n with quant is not renaming — it is grounding the variable
-- in the system that defines it rather than the system being replaced.
-- Status: FORM — this file uses quant throughout.

-- RULE 7: COND-GATE
-- Every conditional in a .hm file must use cond(A, B) (casting.hm § 4),
-- not classical if-then syntax.
-- Classical if-then is material implication — truth-functional, vacuously true
-- when the antecedent is false. cond(A, B) is a structural gate —
-- B does not exist until A has FORM-status.
-- These are not equivalent. Substituting classical if-then hides the FRAME status
-- of unverified preconditions.
-- Status: FORM — this file uses cond where conditionality is invoked.

-- RULE 8: FILE-BOUNDARY-IS-LAYER-BOUNDARY
-- Each .hm file is exactly one formal layer.
-- The + operator (definitive-expansion, axioms.hm § 7) operates between layers:
-- file A + file B means file B expands file A from its end.
-- additionally operates within one file: two forms co-present in the same layer.
-- No file may contain forms from two different layers as if they are co-present
-- without an explicit + join and a new file boundary.
-- Status: FORM — this file is one layer (verification rules). It imports from
-- axioms.hm and casting.hm via the presupposes header (+ inter-layer join).

-- RULE 9: NO-SELF-ATTESTATION
-- No derive block may claim FORM-status by asserting it.
-- The close: line earns the relation through the steps.
-- A derive block that ends in "therefore this is FORM" without having
-- performed a cast through the derivation is self-attestation.
-- Self-attestation is the primary error mode: a FRAME that believes
-- it is FORM because it says so propagates unverified structure into
-- every downstream derivation.
-- Status: FORM — every close line in this file follows from the numbered steps.

-- RULE 10: ABSENCE-IS-NOT-FALSE
-- When a derivation returns absent (casting.hm § 2c, conditionality § 4),
-- the file must not treat absent as false or as a Boolean ⊥.
-- Absent is structural non-presence: the form is not yet derivable,
-- not that it is false.
-- A file that converts absent to false is importing Boolean logic
-- without derivation (violation of RULE 5).
-- Status: FORM — absent appears in this file only in rule descriptions,
-- not as a Boolean value.

-- ================================================================
-- § 2. The Verification Derive Block
-- ================================================================
--
-- This section performs the casting operation on verify.hm itself —
-- applying each rule to this file and confirming it holds.

derive verify-self-check as FORM:
    step 1: RULE 1 (CLOSE-LINE):
            Every derive block in this file ends with close: relation(X).
            Confirming: verify-self-check ends at close: relation(=~).
            All other derive blocks in this file follow the same pattern.
            Rule 1: HOLDS.

    step 2: RULE 2 (FRAME-NAMING):
            Open FRAMEs in this file:
            (a) The kernel implementation (§ 0, stated): absent because
                a running .hm checker does not yet exist.
                Closure criterion: write a parser for .hm syntax that
                enforces rules 1-10 and reports violations.
                Why not closed: no implementation file exists yet.
            Rule 2: HOLDS (FRAME named with all three components).

    step 3: RULE 3 (FILTRATION-INTEGRITY):
            This derive block earns =~ through steps 1-9 which each
            confirm a rule holds. Each step establishes structural
            co-presence (additionally) of the rule and the file's form.
            The accumulated co-presence reaches =~ (full continuation
            capacity of the rule-set is preserved in this file).
            Rule 3: HOLDS.

    step 4: RULE 4 (ETC):
            All introduced terms in this file: "suite", "kernel", "rule",
            "half-cast", "filtration-integrity", "quant-sweep", "cond-gate" —
            each is self-describing from English roots in context.
            "suite" — French suite (sequence): a sequence of related pieces.
            "kernel" — Old French kernel (seed of a nut): the core that
            everything else wraps around. Here: the core checker.
            Rule 4: HOLDS.

    step 5: RULE 5 (NO-IMPORT-WITHOUT-DERIVATION):
            Symbols in this file: cast, cond, ~~, =~, ==, +, additionally,
            quant, quanta_0, absent — all derived in axioms.hm or casting.hm.
            No ∈, ∀, ∃, ℕ, or classical if-then appears.
            Rule 5: HOLDS.

    step 6: RULE 6 (QUANT-SWEEP):
            No bare n, i, j, k appears as a ranging variable.
            The only ranging variable used is quant where needed.
            Rule 6: HOLDS.

    step 7: RULE 7 (COND-GATE):
            No classical if-then connective is used in this file.
            Conditionality in the rule descriptions uses the word "iff"
            in comment text only — not as a formal operator invocation.
            All formal conditionality uses cond(A, B).
            Rule 7: HOLDS.

    step 8: RULE 8 (FILE-BOUNDARY-IS-LAYER-BOUNDARY):
            This file is one layer: the verification layer.
            axioms.hm and casting.hm are prior layers joined by +.
            The presupposes header is the formal statement of the + join.
            No content from two different layers appears as co-present
            without the + inter-layer join.
            Rule 8: HOLDS.

    step 9: RULE 9 (NO-SELF-ATTESTATION):
            Each rule confirmation above (steps 1-8) names the specific
            evidence in this file that satisfies the rule.
            No step says "this rule clearly holds" without pointing at
            the structural evidence.
            Rule 9: HOLDS.

    additionally:
            RULE 10 (ABSENCE-IS-NOT-FALSE):
            The word "absent" appears in this file in rule descriptions
            and in step 2's FRAME naming only.
            It is never converted to a Boolean false.
            Rule 10: HOLDS.

            RULE 11 (MÖBIUS-LINK-VALIDITY):
            This file's presupposes declarations are bulk (~~-level imports).
            No individual links are declared in this file's body.
            No hidden ring structures are introduced.
            All FRAMEs in this file are named with forward-link intent.
            Rule 11: HOLDS.

            RULE 12 (ORDINAL_REFLECTION-GATE):
            This file makes no commutativity claims in its body
            beyond referencing axioms.hm's additionally-is-commutative,
            which is derived with full ordinal_reflection evidence.
            Rule 12: HOLDS.

    close: relation(=~)
    -- verify.hm survives casting through its own rules at =~.
    -- Full congruence (==) would require the kernel implementation
    -- to run this file through an actual checker and return the same structure.
    -- That is the FRAME named in step 2 above.
    -- == closure: deferred to the kernel implementation file.

-- RULE 11: MÖBIUS-LINK-VALIDITY
-- Every link declared in a .hm file must satisfy one of two conditions:
--   (a) Möbius-valid: satisfies all four conditions in links.hm § 3.
--       The link is non-trivial, one-sided, preserves non-commutativity.
--   (b) Explicitly declared as a forward-link.
--       A forward-link targets a FRAME form. Its ordinal_reflection
--       is provisional ~~ until the target achieves FORM.
--       The provisional status must be named — not hidden.
-- A link that is neither Möbius-valid nor declared forward is a hidden
-- ring structure: it claims commutativity without earning it.
-- Status: FORM — this file's presupposes links are bulk (~~-level, acceptable)
--         and its FRAMEs are named. No undeclared forward-links.

-- RULE 12: ORDINAL_REFLECTION-GATE
-- When two operations or links are claimed to commute
-- (order of application does not affect result),
-- that claim must be supported by a derive block showing
-- ordinal_reflection(L1, L2) closes at ==.
-- Commutativity claimed without ordinal_reflection evidence
-- is borrowing classical algebra without derivation (RULE 5 violation).
-- additionally is exempt: its commutativity is derived in axioms.hm.
-- Status: FORM — this file makes no commutativity claims beyond additionally.

-- ================================================================
-- § 3. The Kernel FRAME — What Closes This File to ==
-- ================================================================
--
-- FRAME: kernel implementation
-- What is absent: a parser and checker program that reads .hm files,
--   enforces rules 1-10, and reports violations as named FRAMEs.
-- Closure criterion: write kernel.py (or kernel.hm if self-hosting)
--   that parses the .hm syntax and runs each rule as a structural check.
--   When the kernel runs verify.hm through rules 1-10 and reports
--   no violations, verify.hm achieves == with its own rule-set.
-- Why not closed yet: the kernel does not exist as executable code.
--   The rules are stated in derivation form, not in an executable grammar.
-- Next step: define the .hm grammar formally (what constitutes a valid
--   derive block, a valid close line, a valid FRAME declaration).
--   Then implement the parser. Then run it.
-- Status: FRAME. Continuation capacity is fully preserved (=~ holds).
--   The path through the futures (== level) requires the kernel run.
